-- ===================== GAPS =====================
-- A GAP is a scenario of ANSWERS.md for which the encoding exports no function that can be asked the question, or can be asked but cannot say the answer.
-- Sixty scenarios, in 25 lines below (the numbers are ANSWERS.md scenario numbers); no placeholder assertion was written for a function that does not exist.
-- These were gaps in the encoding as the reviewer received it; each line is the reviewer's account, taken from its triage.
-- GAP 43. "public notice" has the same meaning as "public notification": The synonym exists only inside a glossary string; `is public notification` is named for the first form.
-- GAP 60, 61, 62. s13 "Act": of the Parliament of New Zealand, of the General Assembly, a regulation is not: Glossary string only, no function.
-- GAP 63. s13 "Gazette", including a supplement: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 64. s13 "Commonwealth country", including a territory for whose relations a member is responsible: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 65. s13 "enactment": part of an Act yes, a Bill no: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 66. s13 "office" includes a position: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 67. s13 "Governor-General in Council": acting alone no, on advice and consent yes: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 73. s13 "writing": words shown only on a screen are not covered: `is writing` returns a plain BOOLEAN, so it cannot say "not covered"; the seat question ST7 only reaches Y and N.
-- GAP 74. s13 "prescribed": by regulations yes, by private contract no: Glossary string only.
-- GAP 80. s5 "minimum legislative information": Title, empowering provision, administering agency, regulation-required items yes; date of making no: The four-limb s80(2) evidence function exists; the s5 member test does not.
-- GAP 128. s25(3): a reference in the commencement section to "the rest of this Act" does not include the Title and commencement sections: The commencement function takes a kind of provision and cannot be asked what a phrase in the commencement section refers to.
-- GAP 151, 448. commencement "10 days after" assent or "a year after": not covered: `the day of commencement n months after` takes months only; there is nothing to call to obtain "not covered" for days or years.
-- GAP 157 to 162. s2(1) to (3): this Act's own commencement (s147 on the day after assent; cl 19, s6 for cl 19 and s147A on the day after the Secondary Legislation Act 2021 assent; the rest on Order in Council dates or the fifth anniversary; OIC is secondary legislation): No function for the Act's own commencement.
-- GAP 163. s24: is s24(1) a rule or "a guide only": No function; the text labels it a guide, which is a statement about the text, not a rule.
-- GAP 181. s39: repealed "from" a day: not covered: The repeal functions take "on" and "until" only.
-- GAP 193. s45(c)(iv): a person incapacitated in a way that does not affect their duty: another may not be appointed on that ground: The only available input is the catch-all `another power`, which answers `NOTHING`; "no" cannot be said.
-- GAP 254. Sched 2 cl 3(1)(c): which languages are "official New Zealand languages": not covered: Nothing to call; the term is undefined in the text.
-- GAP 282. s76(1) and (2): the CPC must ensure links are published electronically and give directions: The encoder's own comment above `a direction of the Chief Parliamentary Counsel is secondary legislation` mentions (1) and (2) and states no function for them.
-- GAP 365 to 368. s128(2) and (3), s130: the PCO is an instrument of the Crown, whose control, its seven functions: Part 6 has functions only for ss 131, 133, 134, 146, 147, 147A.
-- GAP 378 to 391. ss 135 to 145: Chief Parliamentary Counsel's status, qualification, term, removal, appointment of parliamentary counsel, remuneration, employer rights, collective agreements, merit: No functions.
-- GAP 394, 395, 396. s147: who may make regulations, the heads of regulation (a) to (t), exemption conditions (2) to (4), discretionary publication requests (7): No functions beyond cost recovery and the notice-is-secondary-legislation rule.
-- GAP 439. Sched 1 cl 35: a Chief Parliamentary Counsel holding office under the 2012 Act continues under s135: Not encoded; the encoder's own comment calls it "machinery".
-- GAP 444, 445. Sched 1A, Sched 3 rows, Sched 4 columns: whether a named empowering provision is listed: The rendering lost the rows; there is nothing in the text to check, and the encoder holds only the opening rule and the grounds.
-- GAP 446. the first anniversary of a 29 February: No function asks it (the test file comment at S446 says so).
-- GAP 449 to 456, 458. silences: the calendar date of Waitangi Day or Easter, whether a given day is a sitting day, when the CPC's term starts, days in the ordinary course of post, whether an email is deemed sent, the date of assent to this Act, a disclosure statement's content (Part 4, s104, heading only), which Order in Council dates apply: These are inputs to the encoding by design (a calendar, a sitting-day list, an assent date); nothing exists to ask, so the "not covered" side is carried by the other silence assertions (S172c, S183b, S192b, S200b, S297b, S310c, S447, ST8 and the `NOTHING` rows in the seat group).
